<!--
  Sysmon ProcessAccess (Event ID 10) block, the FIRST approach I tried for
  detecting credential dumping (LSASS access), kept here for transparency.

  Goal:
    Log when any process opens a handle to lsass.exe with the access rights
    typically requested by credential-dumping tools (0x1010 / 0x1410 / 0x143A).

  Why it is NOT the detection that shipped:
    In this lab the ProcessAccess channel did not reliably produce event 10
    records for the LSASS access (see screenshots/09-wazuh-lsass-gap.png:
    a Wazuh search for data.win.system.eventID: "10" returned "No results").
    Rather than depend on a signal that was not firing, I pivoted to a
    command-line rule (see local_rules.xml) which is a standard and more
    reliable detection method. This file documents the approach that was
    attempted first.

  Usage (reference):
    This block goes inside the <ProcessAccess onmatch="include"> section of a
    Sysmon configuration. The SwiftOnSecurity config was the baseline used.
-->
<ProcessAccess onmatch="include">
  <!-- Flag handles opened against LSASS with dump-capable access masks -->
  <TargetImage condition="image">lsass.exe</TargetImage>
  <GrantedAccess condition="is">0x1010</GrantedAccess>
  <GrantedAccess condition="is">0x1410</GrantedAccess>
  <GrantedAccess condition="is">0x143a</GrantedAccess>
</ProcessAccess>
